  "readmeFile": "README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "1.0\n- Initial release.\n- Added ValidationBehavior: how much a validator reports, along two axes — across its properties\n  (ValidationBehaviors.Class) and within one property's rule chain (ValidationBehaviors.Property).\n  Configurable on the registration, on a validator, and per chain with WithValidationBehavior.\n  Replaces ContinueOnFailure(), which is equivalent to WithValidationBehavior(ValidationBehavior.All).\n  The defaults are unchanged behaviour: every property, one message each.\n- Added NValidation.Testing: assertions over a validation result for unit tests, shipped in the same\n  package and depending on no test framework and no assertion library. result.ShouldReport(\"Vin\",\n  \"Vin is required.\") states the whole expected result — nothing else may be present, the count is\n  implied, order is ignored — and the same overloads take a ValidationException or a\n  { code: [messages] } dictionary. An expected message is matched with wildcards, so a fragment is as\n  easy to express as the whole of it. A failure throws ValidationAssertionException naming what was\n  missing, what was unexpected, and the near miss in between.\n  Also TestValidator<T>, which declares the rule under test in the test itself and takes an optional\n  IValidationMessageProvider; MessageKeyProvider, for assertions independent of any translation;\n  TestTimeProvider, for the date rules; and ShouldResolveEveryCoreMessageKey, which holds an\n  application's own message provider to every key this core can report.",
